<p>
  When your workouts record heart rate, Allure can tell you how hard each one
  was and how your training adds up over weeks. It all starts from your
  heart-rate profile.
</p>

<h2>Your heart-rate profile and zones</h2>
<p>
  In <%= link_to "Heart rate", settings_heart_rate_path %> you give your
  maximum heart rate and, if you know it, your resting heart rate. Allure
  proposes the highest rate one of your recordings held for at least ten
  seconds, which is often a better guess than a formula. From that profile come
  five <strong>zones</strong>, Z1 (easy) to Z5 (maximal), either as percentages
  of your maximum or of your heart-rate reserve (between resting and maximum).
  You can move each threshold yourself.
</p>
<p>
  Changing your profile recomputes your whole history with the new zones.
</p>

<h2>Time in zones</h2>
<p>
  Each workout with heart rate gets a <strong>Heart-rate zones</strong> tab:
  the time spent in each zone. A recording where the heart rate is too sparse,
  or present for less than half the time, gets no zones, and the tab says why.
  Values a slipping strap keeps repeating are set aside rather than counted.
</p>

<h2>Load</h2>
<p>
  The <strong>load</strong> of a workout is the minutes spent in each zone,
  multiplied by the zone's number (the Edwards method): ten minutes in Z4 count
  40 points, ten minutes in Z1 count 10. Time below the floor of your zones —
  50 % of your maximum with the default thresholds — does not count.
</p>

<h2>Fitness, fatigue and form</h2>
<p>
  On your page, the <strong>Training load</strong> card follows three figures
  over the last 90 days, all sports together:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fitness</strong> — your average daily load over 42 days, recent
    days weighing more. What you have built.</li>
  <li><strong>Fatigue</strong> — the same average over 7 days. What you are
    absorbing.</li>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — yesterday's fitness minus yesterday's fatigue.
    Positive, you are fresh; negative, you are loading, which is normal in the
    middle of a training block.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Both averages start from zero at your first workout with heart rate: fitness
  only means something after about six weeks.
</p>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("me.training_load.how")[4] %>
</div>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("me.training_load.how")[5] %>
</div>

<h2>Zones over time</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Heart-rate statistics", heart_rate_stats_path %> stacks your time
  in zones per week or per month, in hours or in shares, and groups it into low,
  moderate and high intensity. Use it as a landmark, not a prescription.
</p>

<h2>It stays private</h2>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("settings.heart_rate.privacy") %>
</div>
<p>
  A publishing profile can share a workout's average and maximum heart rate,
  only if you tick them.
</p>
